example.paragraph:
Let $A = \left\lbrack \begin{array}{rr}
      3 & {- 2} \\
      1 & 0
      \end{array} \right\rbrack$, $\textbf{u} = \left\lbrack \begin{array}{r}
      {- 1} \\
      1
      \end{array} \right\rbrack$, and $\textbf{v} = \left\lbrack \begin{array}{r}
      2 \\
      1
      \end{array} \right\rbrack$. The images of $\textbf{u}$ and v under multiplication by $A$ are shown in Figure 1. In fact, $A\textbf{v}$ is just $2\textbf{v}$. So $A$ only “stretches” or dilates v.

Let $A = \left\lbrack \begin{array}{rr}
      1 & 6 \\
      5 & 2
      \end{array} \right\rbrack$, $\textbf{u} = \left\lbrack \begin{array}{r}
      6 \\
      {- 5}
      \end{array} \right\rbrack$, and $\textbf{v} = \left\lbrack \begin{array}{r}
      3 \\
      {- 2}
      \end{array} \right\rbrack$. Are $\textbf{u}$ and v eigenvectors of $A$?

paragraph.math:
$$\begin{bmatrix}
      x_{1} \\
      x_{2} \\
      x_{3}
      \end{bmatrix}~ = x_{2~}\begin{bmatrix}
      {1/2} \\
      1 \\
      0
      \end{bmatrix}~ + x_{3}~\left\lbrack \begin{array}{r}
      {- 3} \\
      0 \\
      1
      \end{array} \right\rbrack~,\quad x_{2}\text{~and~}x_{3}\text{~free}$$

note.paragraph:
Remember that once you find a potential eigenvector $\textbf{v}$, you can easily check your answer: just find $A\textbf{v}$ and see if it is a multiple of $\textbf{v}$. For example, to check whether $\textbf{v} = \left\lbrack \begin{array}{r}
      1 \\
      1
      \end{array} \right\rbrack$ is an eigenvector of $A = \left\lbrack \begin{array}{rr}
      1 & 2 \\
      {- 1} & {- 2}
      \end{array} \right\rbrack$, notice $A\textbf{v} = \left\lbrack \begin{array}{r}
      3 \\
      {- 3}
      \end{array} \right\rbrack$, which is not a multiple of $\textbf{v} = \left\lbrack \begin{array}{r}
      1 \\
      1
      \end{array} \right\rbrack$, establishing that $\textbf{v}$ is not an eigenvector. It turns out we had a sign error. The vector $\textbf{u} = \left\lbrack \begin{array}{r}
      1 \\
      {- 1}
      \end{array} \right\rbrack$ is a correct eigenvector for $A$ since $A\textbf{u} = \left\lbrack \begin{array}{r}
      {- 1} \\
      1
      \end{array} \right\rbrack = - 1\left\lbrack \begin{array}{r}
      1 \\
      {- 1}
      \end{array} \right\rbrack = - 1\,\textbf{u}$.

paragraph.math:
$$\begin{matrix}
      {A - \text{λ}I} & {= ~\begin{bmatrix}
      a_{11} & a_{12} & a_{13} \\
      0 & a_{22} & a_{23} \\
      0 & 0 & a_{33}
      \end{bmatrix}~ - ~\begin{bmatrix}
      \text{λ} & 0 & 0 \\
      0 & \text{λ} & 0 \\
      0 & 0 & \text{λ}
      \end{bmatrix}} \\
       & {= ~\begin{bmatrix}
      {a_{11} - \text{λ}} & a_{12} & a_{13} \\
      0 & {a_{22} - \text{λ}} & a_{23} \\
      0 & 0 & {a_{33} - \text{λ}}
      \end{bmatrix}}
      \end{matrix}$$

example.paragraph:
Let $A = \begin{bmatrix}
      3 & {~~6} & {- 8} \\
      0 & {~~0} & {~~6} \\
      0 & {~~0} & {~~2}
      \end{bmatrix}$ and $B = \left\lbrack \begin{array}{rrr}
      4 & 0 & 0 \\
      {- 2} & 1 & 0 \\
      5 & 3 & 4
      \end{array} \right\rbrack$. The eigenvalues of $A$ are $3$, $0$, and $2$. The eigenvalues of $B$ are $4$ and $1$.

statement.paragraph:
Is $\text{λ} = - 2$ an eigenvalue of $\left\lbrack \begin{array}{rr}
      7 & 3 \\
      3 & {- 1}
      \end{array} \right\rbrack$? Why or why not?

$A = \left\lbrack \begin{array}{rr}
      10 & {- 9} \\
      4 & {- 2}
      \end{array} \right\rbrack$, $\text{λ} = 4$

Without calculation, find one eigenvalue and two linearly independent eigenvectors of $A = ~\begin{bmatrix}
      5 & {- 5} & {~5} \\
      5 & {- 5} & {~5} \\
      5 & {- 5} & {~5}
      \end{bmatrix}$. Justify your answer.
